1
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:24,120
From the age of 13, I started
stuffing myself with potato chips,

2
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:29,160
pastries and cookies. I’d sit in front
of the TV and just eat more and more.

3
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,720
The ads showed all this
stuff that looked really tasty

4
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:36,560
- and I needed
to try everything.

5
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:44,320
At school, I got picked on a lot about
my weight. I was angry with myself.

6
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:47,840
Why am I gaining so much
weight? Why am I so ugly?

7
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:50,800
Why do people
think I’m a monster?

8
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:57,320
Why do I get insulted in the street by
strangers, just because of how I look?

9
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,800
I was mad at myself for
putting on so much weight

10
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,120
and not being
able to lose it.

11
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:07,680
In movies, people seemed to
have no problem losing weight.

12
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,680
But reality
is different.

13
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,440
Carole is 34 years old and lives
near the Swiss city of Lausanne.

14
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:26,000
Since her teens, her diet has
consisted primarily of sandwiches,

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,560
snacks and sugary
drinks... junk food.

16
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,560
The more I ate,
the worse I felt

17
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:41,240
- including my problems
with how people looked at me.

18
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:43,760
And I ate more
to feel better.

19
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:46,920
It was a vicious circle
that I couldn't escape from.

20
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:50,520
And my "doses"
continually increased.

21
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:55,560
Of things that were sweeter
or saltier or more flavorful.

22
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:58,120
I gradually
lost control.

23
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:07,600
Sugar, salt and fat: three ingredients
that make food irresistible.

24
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:12,560
Scientists working for
manufacturers are constantly trying

25
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:16,440
to find the perfect combination
of these tempting ingredients.

26
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,640
We talked to a man who
knows the industry inside and out,

27
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:25,400
and reveals the strategies
deployed by the food giants.

28
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:31,080
Those three ingredients
are very powerful.

29
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:34,840
Salt, they call it
the flavor burst,

30
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:40,440
because typically, it’s on the
surface of chips, on snacks.

31
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:44,880
It’s the first thing that
touches the tongue.

32
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:51,760
They love fats, as well, because those
are what they call the mouth feel.

33
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:58,160
The sensation of biting into, you
know, a warm, toasted cheese sandwich.

34
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:04,800
And then, probably, the most powerful
of the three for many people, is sugar.

35
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:13,040
Just, by natural, by basic instinct.
And so the industry has engineered

36
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:15,840
what they call
the bliss point,

37
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:18,200
the perfect amount
of sugar in products:

38
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:19,920
not too little,
not too much,

39
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,680
working on maximizing
the allure of their products.

40
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,080
The investigative
journalist spent years

41
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:29,440
researching for his best-selling
book on the food industry

42
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:32,080
and the manufacturing
of processed foods.

43
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:37,320
I resisted calling their
products addictive,

44
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:44,680
because it seemed crazy to me to
compare Oreo cookies with heroin.

45
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:46,440
It just seemed
ludicrous.

46
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:52,160
But I have to say, that several
things completely changed my mind.

47
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:53,960
And I’m now convinced
that in some ways,

48
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:59,000
these food products are
even more problematic for us

49
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,480
than tobacco, alcohol,
even some kinds of drugs.

50
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:10,720
It's no longer a secret that
these highly processed products

51
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:14,440
are directly linked to the
global obesity epidemic.

52
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:36,000
Carole is a patient
at the obesity clinic

53
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,880
of the University
Hospital in Lausanne.

54
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,120
Two years ago, her
obesity almost killed her.

55
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:45,600
She was barely able
to move or even breath.

56
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:48,960
She's recently undergone
gastric bypass surgery,

57
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,920
which drastically reduced
the size of her stomach.

58
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:57,800
Carole's come to the clinic
today to see an endocrinologist

59
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,520
and a psychiatrist.

60
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:04,160
She's already shed 35 kilos,
but has a lot of things to re-learn

61
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:07,680
- such as the sensation
of feeling hungry or full.

62
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:10,880
She also has to learn
self-respect again.

63
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:14,120
She’s got a long
road ahead of her.

64
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:16,560
How have you been?

65
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,560
I don’t feel like there’s been a
change or I’ve lost any weight.

66
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:26,240
In the mirror, I can't
see any difference.

67
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,880
I do see it in the
clothes, though.

68
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:31,320
The shirt I'm wearing
used to be too small for me.

69
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,040
And there are some
pants that were too small

70
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:37,600
that I can wear again, and
others that are now too big!

71
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:42,160
The multidisciplinary department
at Lausanne University Hospital

72
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:45,800
has around 15-hundred
overweight patients on its books.

73
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:49,840
Carole is one of the 220
who suffer from obesity.

74
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,960
We've seen an explosion
in the number of patients

75
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:58,280
suffering from
extreme obesity.

76
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:00,680
In recent decades our
immediate environment

77
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:06,120
has seen changes so profound that
we now call it obesogenic or toxic.

78
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:09,280
And toxic in
terms of calories.

79
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:12,680
Advertising and special
promotional offers encourage us

80
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:17,160
to eat more and more food
- and often of poorer quality.

81
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:20,520
And this compromises
our personal freedom.

82
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:28,200
Here in Switzerland, 42% of
the population is overweight.

83
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:30,560
One in ten
is obese.

84
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:36,040
There's a link between the emergence
of ultra-processed junk food,

85
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:40,520
the decrease in physical activity,
and increase in screen time.

86
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:45,800
And this ensemble of environmental
factors favors the explosion of obesity

87
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,920
that we're
currently seeing.

88
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:53,600
Research papers show
growing indications of the link

89
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:58,440
between obesity and the consumption
of industrially processed foods.

90
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:06,640
Absolutely. Ultra-processed
foods often have calories

91
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:12,440
in extremely large quantities while
not necessarily making you feel full.

92
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:17,600
And in the context of
this explosion in obesity,

93
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,360
these foods clearly
play a major role.

94
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:28,880
A doctor linking unhealthy
food to obesity is understandable.

95
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:33,680
But what we might not expect is
the world's largest food manufacturer

96
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,560
admitting the same.

97
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:41,560
In 2021, Nestlé came under fire after
details from an internal presentation

98
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,400
were published by
the Financial Times.

99
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:46,920
The document showed
the company acknowledging

100
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:49,800
that the majority of its
products were unhealthy.

101
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:54,360
Over 60% did not meet a
“recognized definition of health".

102
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:56,480
The data did not
include coffee,

103
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:01,240
baby formulas and food for
specific medical conditions.

104
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:04,680
The presentation was
intended for company executives

105
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:08,240
as a basis for revising
their corporate strategy.

106
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,760
And the multinational's
response to the revelation?

107
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:17,840
Was it a shock to learn that
far more products than expected

108
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:21,040
were below this threshold
for unhealthy foods?

109
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:28,000
Once we realized this, we
knew we had to do something.

110
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,960
We want to determine which
products we should keep,

111
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:40,360
which ones we discard, and
which ones we could reformulate.

112
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,800
And this is a policy
that you're following

113
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:47,880
because there is a real awareness that
a lot of your foods are unhealthy?

114
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:50,560
Or because you need
to keep selling products

115
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,920
to stay number
one in the world?

116
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:03,080
We want to be there for
consumers, and be where they are.

117
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:07,360
With Covid, there's been growing
concern about health and immunity

118
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:10,000
- and about
sugar intake.

119
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,360
People are now more aware of the
need to reduce their consumption.

120
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:21,080
We want to accompany people on that
journey to eat better and stay healthy.

121
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:26,840
Nestlé has already removed a number
of product lines from its portfolio,

122
00:09:26,840 --> 00:09:28,920
such as sausage
products.

123
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:32,680
But why didn't the Swiss
multinational and other food giants

124
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:34,720
change
course sooner?

125
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:41,120
Minneapolis, USA.
The 8th of April, 1999.

126
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:43,880
A secret meeting
brought together the CEOs

127
00:09:43,880 --> 00:09:49,080
of the country's eight biggest food
companies - among them, Nestlé USA.

128
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:53,480
On the agenda: their role in one of the
most serious public health epidemics

129
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:59,120
ever seen in the US: rising
overweight and obesity rates.

130
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:06,800
There is growing concern about
their culpability of their products

131
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:14,400
to weight gain, to diabetes, to
even several types of cancer.

132
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:18,440
And they get together and one of
them, a gentleman named Michael Mudd,

133
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:21,480
who’s an executive
vice president of Kraft,

134
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:26,320
one of the biggest companies
of all, introduces the crowd,

135
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:30,120
and gets up and
explains the situation.

136
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,480
"We cannot pretend food isn’t
part of the obesity problem...

137
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:36,280
No credible expert will
attribute the rise in obesity

138
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:38,920
solely to decreased
physical activity.

139
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:42,240
The one thing we
shouldn’t do is nothing..."

140
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:51,480
As you can imagine, he was not well
received by the heads of the companies

141
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:56,440
and one of the company executives
gets up and he’s kind of visibly angry

142
00:10:56,440 --> 00:11:01,760
and he goes, you know,
look, there’s no way, he says,

143
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:05,920
that we’re going to mess
around with the company jewels,

144
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:10,240
- referring to their powerful
ingredients like salt, sugar, fat -

145
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:19,240
if that's going to diminish the allure,
the attractiveness of the sales.

146
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:23,920
The corporations saw no reason -
or incentive - to change anything.

147
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:28,200
The meeting ended abruptly -
illustrating their lack of interest.

148
00:11:32,120 --> 00:11:35,200
There was - seemingly - no
stopping the obesity epidemic

149
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:38,720
- as shown by the growing
amount of red on this map.

150
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:44,720
Weight gain is rapidly
increasing - worldwide.

151
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:50,000
The darker the shades of red,
the higher the rates of obesity.

152
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:55,800
Mexico is among the
countries most gravely affected.

153
00:11:55,800 --> 00:11:59,000
Over three-quarters of
the adult population here

154
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,120
are classified as
overweight or obese.

155
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:11,880
Mexico also has one the
worst rates of child obesity.

156
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:21,560
In grocery stores here, the
shelves are packed with junk food.

157
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:27,960
Obesity is prevalent among
all ages of the population...

158
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:33,520
including Rogelio, who mostly eats
chips, other snacks, and sugary drinks.

159
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:39,880
Ever since I was little, I've
been eating this type of food.

160
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:43,120
My mother never told
me to eat fruit instead.

161
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:46,360
She always said: If
you like it, then eat it.

162
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:57,040
Rogelio is
a cab driver.

163
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:01,960
He spends up to 12
hours a day in his car.

164
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:12,320
It's super-tasty and
helps me keep on working.

165
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:15,800
I can keep driving without
having to stop to feed myself.

166
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:33,080
At home, it’s the same. Fast, fatty
and cheap food - washed down with soda.

167
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:39,280
Rogelio has two children: a daughter
in her teens, and a little boy.

168
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:43,480
I'll show you some pictures
where I was a little fat.

169
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:46,560
Me with my son.

170
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:58,840
Here I weighed about 120
kilos... and in this picture, 126 kilos.

171
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:01,920
Was all that
weight a burden?

172
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:06,640
Yes, I was really suffering. You
don't want to go out anymore,

173
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:10,360
because you're
immediately out of breath.

174
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:16,560
Honestly, I was just lying
around and eating continuously.

175
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:22,840
Since then, I've been trying to lose
some weight, so I can live longer.

176
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:30,360
The current government has made
the battle against obesity a priority.

177
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:37,120
And leading the charge...
is Hugo López-Gatell

178
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:42,560
- a doctor, trusted advisor to the
president and deputy minister of health.

179
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:48,480
For at least the
last 15 years,

180
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:54,520
about a third of all deaths in Mexico
have been caused by poor nutrition.

181
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:04,080
And that’s mainly due to excess amounts
of sugar, calories, fat and salt.

182
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:12,000
In 1980, just under 7% of the
population in Mexico was obese.

183
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,640
Since then, the figure
has risen over five-fold.

184
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:25,400
In that same time-frame, the
country has undergone radical changes

185
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:29,520
- due to the opening of the
market to international trade.

186
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:35,880
In the 1980s, there was
a very abrupt change.

187
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:38,280
Both economically
and socially,

188
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:43,120
the country turned towards
neoliberalism and economic deregulation.

189
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:48,400
At the same time, the
protections provided by the state

190
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:51,440
against health risks
were dismantled

191
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:56,240
- although it would take a few years
for the consequences to become apparent.

192
00:15:57,960 --> 00:16:04,240
There was an increase in overweight
people - and after about 10 to 15 years,

193
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:07,160
we were seeing chronic
diseases like hypertension,

194
00:16:07,160 --> 00:16:10,480
diabetes, obesity
and cancer etcetera.

195
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,560
The tipping point was the signing
of the free trade agreements

196
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:19,960
with the US and
Canada in 1994

197
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:24,400
- which flooded the Mexican market
with cheap, processed products.

198
00:16:26,080 --> 00:16:31,800
The ubiquity of sugary drinks, like
Coca-Cola, is symbolic of this disaster.

199
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:39,040
Today Mexicans consume an average of
163 liters of soda per person per year.

200
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:41,080
With action
urgently needed,

201
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:44,560
the Mexican parliament passed
three essential regulations:

202
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:49,600
a sugar tax, a clamp-down
on adverts targeting children,

203
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:53,880
and a warning label prominently
displayed on unhealthy products

204
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:56,680
- similar to
cigarette packaging.

205
00:16:56,680 --> 00:16:59,440
The measures were lobbied
for by consumer groups,

206
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:03,800
doctors and private groups
like the one run by Doré Castillo.

207
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:12,080
Today, manufacturers have to
declare whether their products

208
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:18,200
contain excess sugar, calories,
saturated fat, trans fats, and salt.

209
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:23,600
This is fundamental in the fight
against excess weight, obesity

210
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:26,800
and preventable
non-communicable diseases.

211
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:31,760
To avoid these labels, many brands
have changed product formulas

212
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:36,360
and reduced the amount
of sugar, for example.

213
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:39,720
And then there are there the
restrictions on children's products

214
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:43,320
- in particular the
animal mascots.

215
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:49,320
The manufacturers had to remove
the tigers from these cereal boxes.

216
00:17:49,320 --> 00:17:52,760
Others had an
elephant or a toucan.

217
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:59,840
These images are ingrained in
the memory of so many children

218
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:02,400
- and are all part of
a marketing strategy

219
00:18:02,400 --> 00:18:04,960
to boost sales
and brand loyalty.

220
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:14,000
It's great to see these
labels and different packaging

221
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:21,120
helping people make their
decisions. It was really tough

222
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:23,400
- part of the general
struggle we're fighting

223
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,960
to protect the health
of people in Mexico.

224
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:32,240
Food corporations have
launched legal challenges

225
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:34,520
to oppose the
mandatory labels.

226
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:38,200
They claim that the labels
"prevent consumers from being able

227
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:42,520
to compare products and selecting
those that mean a healthy diet."

228
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:50,480
It's scandalous
that food companies

229
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:53,960
- whether they're multinational,
international or Mexican -

230
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:57,120
are so indifferent
to human suffering.

231
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:04,440
Basically, they have
contempt for ordinary people.

232
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:16,240
I've heard these executives so often,
pretending to talk to the government.

233
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:21,040
But in reality, they
have other intentions:

234
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:25,800
delaying tactics and
misinformation in order to win time

235
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:28,320
and keep on
making profit

236
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:32,600
- despite being aware that they
are killing people, especially minors.

237
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:42,800
We contacted the big players
in the Mexican food industry

238
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:45,800
- but none of them wanted
to give us an interview.

239
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:56,640
As for the situation
in Switzerland...

240
00:19:56,640 --> 00:19:59,280
Here, there are no
legal constraints in place

241
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:02,160
to combat overweight
and obesity rates

242
00:20:02,160 --> 00:20:06,880
- despite them costing over 8 billion
Swiss francs a year in healthcare.

243
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:11,320
How about a tax
on sugary drinks?

244
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:16,200
An idea that's already been adopted
in over 50 nations around the world.

245
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:21,240
Among them are a number of European
countries, including France and Britain.

246
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:23,760
And: it works.

247
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:28,040
We take the example of a bottle
of Fanta bought in a store in Britain,

248
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:31,040
where it's subject
to the special tax.

249
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:35,120
Some of the sugar has been
replaced with a sweetener,

250
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:39,960
reducing the sugar content
to 4-point-6 grams per deciliter.

251
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:45,920
The same drink in Switzerland
contains 10-point-3 grams of sugar

252
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:47,680
- over twice
the amount.

253
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:54,360
That’s right! 4.6 grams
as opposed to 10.3.

254
00:20:54,360 --> 00:20:57,600
It's difficult to tell whether what
you're consuming is healthy.

255
00:20:58,160 --> 00:21:01,400
There are 15 different
names for sugar,

256
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:04,840
and we never know how
high its content actually is.

257
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:10,080
There are marketing tools enabling
companies to hide the sugar content.

258
00:21:10,080 --> 00:21:14,440
The government needs
to take responsibility here.

259
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:18,280
Politician Delphine Bachmann
has submitted an initiative

260
00:21:18,280 --> 00:21:21,640
from the Geneva region for
the Swiss national parliament.

261
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:27,160
The goal is a tax
to limit sugar content

262
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:30,680
in beverages and
processed foods.

263
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:35,000
The industry knows full well
that the products are unhealthy.

264
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,880
Companies know
they're hiding sugar,

265
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:40,000
but they're afraid to
lose these consumers

266
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:46,240
who've become accustomed to the
products. They don't want transparency.

267
00:21:46,240 --> 00:21:48,600
What we need
is political action,

268
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:50,920
and there are signs
of that in a few regions.

269
00:21:51,800 --> 00:21:55,160
My hope is that the
accumulation of political moves

270
00:21:55,160 --> 00:22:00,000
will push our authorities into
action - and also raise awareness

271
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:04,760
among the population about these
issues... and bring about change.

272
00:22:07,640 --> 00:22:11,520
Over the last few years, a series
of motions have been submitted,

273
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:14,640
calling for state
intervention - to no avail.

274
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:17,120
While a sugar tax is
not yet on the agenda,

275
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:22,120
the parliament is planning possible
restrictions on advertising to children.

276
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:27,240
A majority of parliament does
not want market regulation

277
00:22:27,240 --> 00:22:29,760
for ideological
reasons.

278
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:33,640
And there are lobby groups linked
to sugar production and processing,

279
00:22:33,640 --> 00:22:38,680
and the food industry, who
have friends in parliament.

280
00:22:39,640 --> 00:22:43,200
Lobbies are important sources
of expertise and information.

281
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:46,600
But when industry
interests become dominant

282
00:22:46,600 --> 00:22:51,160
and influence public health
and spending, that's not OK.

283
00:22:51,160 --> 00:22:54,400
We show this politician
the soda from Switzerland

284
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:57,880
containing twice the sugar
content as the British version.

285
00:22:57,880 --> 00:23:02,160
...and the World Bank report
on the impact of the sugar tax.

286
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:05,200
"...The taxation of
sugar-sweetened beverages

287
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:08,720
is internationally recommended
as a priority component

288
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:12,840
of a comprehensive approach to
preventing and controlling obesity."

289
00:23:15,960 --> 00:23:20,320
The World Bank report speaks
volumes - and is encouraging reading.

290
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:23,320
It clearly says that hidden
sugars are a disaster,

291
00:23:23,320 --> 00:23:25,680
and that the sugar
content has to be reduced

292
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:29,080
in the interest of public health
concerns and public spending.

293
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:35,280
But that’s not going to be of interest
for the majority of parliament.

294
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:38,280
How does parliament
explain their resistance?

295
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:44,960
We put that question
to 10 Swiss politicians

296
00:23:44,960 --> 00:23:48,880
who are also members of a soft-drinks
lobby group representing the likes

297
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:53,520
of Coca-Cola, Red Bull and
domestic sugary beverage suppliers.

298
00:23:56,720 --> 00:23:59,440
A statement by
the group claims:

299
00:23:59,440 --> 00:24:02,080
"...We are active and
transparent participants

300
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:05,080
in the political
and social debate."

301
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:06,080
And yet:

302
00:24:06,080 --> 00:24:10,720
of the ten members of parliament
we asked, none agreed to an interview.

303
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:16,560
For consumers facing a
constant flood of advertising,

304
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:19,960
making an informed choice
isn’t so straightforward...

305
00:24:19,960 --> 00:24:22,720
as we see at this
Geneva school.

306
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:28,040
A presentation teaches pupils
about the amounts of sugar and fat

307
00:24:28,040 --> 00:24:29,720
in processed foods.

308
00:24:29,720 --> 00:24:33,520
Over here are products
that, ideally, they should avoid.

309
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:37,840
On the other side are healthy
options - the kinds of foods

310
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:43,720
they should be consuming instead
of potato chips and sugary drinks.

311
00:24:43,720 --> 00:24:47,040
The children show us
what they normally eat.

312
00:24:52,760 --> 00:24:55,880
Can you show us
what you have there?

313
00:24:56,720 --> 00:24:58,720
Wow!

314
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:05,080
Since its introduction 15 years
ago, the program has spread...

315
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:08,880
and is now being used by
one in three Geneva schools.

316
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:15,280
Eating behavior is
formed in early childhood,

317
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:19,480
before the age of 10, when
most of our habits develop.

318
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:24,480
The food industry has
understood this very well

319
00:25:24,480 --> 00:25:27,160
and targets children
with aggressive marketing

320
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:31,920
to get them used to ultra-processed
foods and neatly-packaged snacks.

321
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:39,480
Which of these things would you
buy first when you’re in a store?

322
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:47,640
Kinder Bueno, candies,
M&amp;Ms and apples.

323
00:25:49,400 --> 00:25:54,240
Chips, waffles, candies
and M&amp;Ms. That's all.

324
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:55,480
Why?

325
00:25:56,480 --> 00:25:58,080
They look tasty!

326
00:25:58,960 --> 00:26:00,440
And you?

327
00:26:00,440 --> 00:26:03,080
Everything
over here!

328
00:26:04,360 --> 00:26:08,240
Most people have cravings
for sweet, salty and fatty foods.

329
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:10,120
But for some,
like Rebecca,

330
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:13,800
these cravings can become
a serious health problem.

331
00:26:16,360 --> 00:26:20,880
For 20 years she's been suffering
from a compulsive eating disorder

332
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:23,320
- binge-eating on
a regular basis,

333
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:27,560
consuming enormous amounts
in a very short period of time.

334
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:42,600
It became a habit. When the emotions
got too much, food became my refuge.

335
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:46,320
Sometimes it felt like a
complete disaster inside me

336
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:48,680
- and this was the
only way I could cope.

337
00:26:56,240 --> 00:26:58,240
Rebecca has
been out shopping

338
00:26:58,240 --> 00:27:00,760
- to show us what she
would typically consume

339
00:27:00,760 --> 00:27:05,680
when emotional stress led to binge-eating.
A courageous step on her part.

340
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:10,680
So you'd eat all of
this in one sitting?

341
00:27:10,680 --> 00:27:14,680
Maybe not everything,
but most of it.

342
00:27:16,680 --> 00:27:21,400
And how do you proceed?
What would you start with?

343
00:27:22,760 --> 00:27:25,480
...The ravioli.

344
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:27,920
The ravioli
with meat?

345
00:27:27,920 --> 00:27:31,480
Yes. With
cream sauce.

346
00:27:31,480 --> 00:27:32,480
How much?

347
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:36,840
...The whole pack.
Plus grated cheese.

348
00:27:36,840 --> 00:27:40,280
Also the
whole pack?

349
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:42,480
Yes. Everything
has to go!

350
00:27:44,200 --> 00:27:47,360
And then? You
carry on eating?

351
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:50,640
It depends on how much
room I still have in my stomach!

352
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:59,720
These I would've eaten on
the way here... the whole lot.

353
00:28:01,440 --> 00:28:09,960
Then perhaps a couple of madeleines.
And then some cookies - the whole pack.

354
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:15,600
And how do
you feel then?

355
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:20,920
Not good. The first few
bites are always nice.

356
00:28:20,920 --> 00:28:24,920
You have the taste... and you
enjoy eating these products.

357
00:28:24,920 --> 00:28:27,920
But after a while,
there's no more pleasure.

358
00:28:30,280 --> 00:28:34,760
And it's then simply not
possible for you to stop eating?

359
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:40,120
...No. I don't stop until
I have to, physically...

360
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:44,160
when the stomach-ache is so
bad that I can't sit or lie down.

361
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:50,520
And you feel
ashamed?

362
00:28:50,520 --> 00:28:55,840
Yes, absolutely. That's
why I do it in secret.

363
00:28:55,840 --> 00:29:00,480
I feel ashamed, because nobody's
asking me to eat all of this stuff,

364
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:02,720
in these excessive
quantities.

365
00:29:07,440 --> 00:29:10,200
Why are you telling
us about this now?

366
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:14,520
Saying it out loud
is part of my journey

367
00:29:14,520 --> 00:29:19,400
- acknowledging it and admitting
to myself that I have a problem.

368
00:29:19,400 --> 00:29:23,600
I'm trying to find a
way out and a solution.

369
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:27,240
And I want to tell
people that this isn't easy.

370
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:31,000
It's not just a
question of willpower.

371
00:29:33,760 --> 00:29:37,360
A lot of it takes place
behind closed doors.

372
00:29:44,360 --> 00:29:47,280
Rebecca also sought
help from the obesity clinic

373
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:49,600
at Lausanne
University Hospital.

374
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:53,640
She's now working with a psychiatrist
on her relationship to food,

375
00:29:53,640 --> 00:29:56,360
in order to prevent
future binges.

376
00:29:58,440 --> 00:30:01,720
So how have you been
doing since last week?

377
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:03,200
Good.

378
00:30:06,280 --> 00:30:11,320
The intake of food is extremely
quick, and the foods are often those

379
00:30:11,320 --> 00:30:14,120
that generate a feeling
of excitement, and craving.

380
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:18,440
In many cases:
sweet, fatty foods.

381
00:30:18,440 --> 00:30:24,400
It's at this point that they lose
control, and are unable to stop.

382
00:30:27,400 --> 00:30:31,480
Countless experiments have been
conducted on people losing control

383
00:30:31,480 --> 00:30:34,320
when consuming
particular substances.

384
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:37,440
This rat is quick to learn
that pressing a pedal

385
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:42,320
will reward it with a dose of sugar
- which it then eats frantically.

386
00:30:42,320 --> 00:30:47,320
A similar experiment showed that rats
prefer consuming sugar to cocaine.

387
00:30:54,760 --> 00:30:58,880
The power of sugar is far more
important than previously thought

388
00:30:58,880 --> 00:31:02,720
- as was confirmed
by this experiment.

389
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:09,200
Benjamin Boutrel heads a
neurobiological research unit

390
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:13,400
for addictive and eating disorders
at Lausanne University Hospital.

391
00:31:13,400 --> 00:31:18,320
His area of focus is the mechanism
behind addiction and loss of control.

392
00:31:21,680 --> 00:31:26,520
We see changes in the brain of someone
who cannot control their intake

393
00:31:26,520 --> 00:31:28,600
of fatty and
sugary foods...

394
00:31:30,600 --> 00:31:32,160
And they're comparable
to those seen

395
00:31:32,160 --> 00:31:36,200
with excessive and uncontrolled
consumption of alcohol,

396
00:31:36,200 --> 00:31:38,280
cocaine or tobacco.

397
00:31:40,760 --> 00:31:45,080
We can draw a parallel
between the agri-food industry

398
00:31:45,080 --> 00:31:46,920
and the
tobacco industry

399
00:31:46,920 --> 00:31:50,920
- in terms of the way they've
optimized their products,

400
00:31:52,960 --> 00:31:56,960
and how they've changed
the very nature of their product:

401
00:31:56,960 --> 00:31:59,600
in this case,
ultra-processing.

402
00:31:59,600 --> 00:32:02,840
Tobacco companies
add texturizers and flavors

403
00:32:02,840 --> 00:32:07,520
to optimize the nicotine efficacy.
The objective in both cases

404
00:32:07,520 --> 00:32:11,480
is to retain their consumers
and increase sales.

405
00:32:21,440 --> 00:32:24,040
Journalist Michael Moss,
who has dubbed sugar,

406
00:32:24,040 --> 00:32:26,640
salt and fat an
unholy trinity,

407
00:32:26,640 --> 00:32:30,840
wanted to understand how US
food engineers develop products,

408
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:34,000
and investigated many
industry laboratories.

409
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:36,720
The industry hates
the word addictive.

410
00:32:36,720 --> 00:32:40,560
You won't see them
in their laboratories,

411
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:43,120
talking to each
other, like,

412
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:47,000
How do we make this new
potato chip more addictive?

413
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:50,960
They use other terms
to describe their efforts

414
00:32:50,960 --> 00:32:53,360
to maximize the
allure of their products.

415
00:32:53,360 --> 00:32:59,560
They talk about engineering
"crave-ability" for example, right.

416
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:04,840
They talk about engineering,
well, on of my favorite expressions,

417
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:09,160
is "moreishness",
right, as in,

418
00:33:09,160 --> 00:33:13,680
getting us to want more
and more of their product.

419
00:33:14,760 --> 00:33:19,600
Are manufacturers deliberately
targeting vulnerable consumers?

420
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:25,480
The industry has a formula which
they call, sort of, the 80/20 rule,

421
00:33:25,480 --> 00:33:33,160
which is that 20% of their customers
will be eating 80% of the product.

422
00:33:33,160 --> 00:33:36,560
So: a few people
eating a whole lot.

423
00:33:36,560 --> 00:33:40,440
They call those
people the heavy users.

424
00:33:40,440 --> 00:33:44,920
And they will target
them through marketing,

425
00:33:44,920 --> 00:33:50,000
knowing that those
people for various reasons

426
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:53,040
are vulnerable to
overeating their products.

427
00:33:53,040 --> 00:33:55,960
If they focus on the
20% of customers

428
00:33:55,960 --> 00:34:00,480
who are eating 80% of the product,
they’re going to maximize their sales.

429
00:34:01,240 --> 00:34:05,080
What about Nestlé, the
world's biggest food corporation?

430
00:34:05,080 --> 00:34:08,240
Is it targeting people
prone to excessive eating?

431
00:34:08,240 --> 00:34:11,360
And why does the company
continue marketing products

432
00:34:11,360 --> 00:34:15,560
that it has itself called unhealthy?
During a one-hour interview,

433
00:34:15,560 --> 00:34:18,240
we repeatedly asked
questions like these

434
00:34:18,240 --> 00:34:21,280
- and were given almost
always the same answer.

435
00:34:24,440 --> 00:34:27,320
Nestlé wants to help
people to eat well,

436
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:32,080
which means finding the right
balance between enjoyment and health.

437
00:34:33,200 --> 00:34:36,760
Nestlé's philosophy is to
help consumers eat well,

438
00:34:36,760 --> 00:34:41,560
combining enjoyment with a
balanced diet, and sound health.

439
00:34:42,840 --> 00:34:46,120
And that's why Nestlé offers
both a range of affordable

440
00:34:46,120 --> 00:34:49,800
and nutritious products,
and gourmet products

441
00:34:49,800 --> 00:34:52,280
that should be
consumed in moderation.

442
00:34:56,800 --> 00:35:01,640
But it's known in practice that some
of your consumers are heavy users

443
00:35:01,640 --> 00:35:04,240
- people who
eat excessively.

444
00:35:06,680 --> 00:35:11,080
Aren't these consumers
essentially addicted to your products?

445
00:35:14,040 --> 00:35:18,880
That's why we've been introducing
educational programs. So...

446
00:35:19,840 --> 00:35:22,440
But do you think
they're addicted?

447
00:35:24,880 --> 00:35:30,120
Addicted? No, I don't think so.
Not to Nestlé products, at least.

448
00:35:30,120 --> 00:35:33,440
Our products are not
developed with that in mind.

449
00:35:33,440 --> 00:35:37,840
We develop our products to
fit a balanced and healthy diet.

450
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:45,000
...Each manufacturer has its own
strategy for seducing our appetites.

451
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:48,880
Michael Moss has no doubt that
the most vulnerable consumers

452
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:52,960
are key to the food
giants' market success.

453
00:35:52,960 --> 00:35:56,800
In the sense of defining
the word addiction

454
00:35:56,800 --> 00:35:59,080
as being kind of this
repetitive behavior

455
00:35:59,080 --> 00:36:02,600
that some people
find difficult to quit,

456
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:06,720
I think that absolutely
defines the business model

457
00:36:06,720 --> 00:36:09,240
of the processed
food industry.

458
00:36:09,800 --> 00:36:16,000
Spending every waking hour engineering
and marketing their products

459
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:23,280
in a way that causes us not just to
love them, but to want more and more.

460
00:36:25,120 --> 00:36:28,120
So what action is
Switzerland taking?

461
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:32,560
In 2020 the government stated
its opposition to the law in Mexico

462
00:36:32,560 --> 00:36:34,600
requiring food
manufacturers

463
00:36:34,600 --> 00:36:38,120
to declare excessive
levels of sugar, fat and salt.

464
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:41,360
They do so using these
black warning labels.

465
00:36:43,200 --> 00:36:46,280
What prompted
Switzerland to get involved?

466
00:36:46,280 --> 00:36:50,240
Several dozen internal e-mails obtained
thanks to the country's freedom

467
00:36:50,240 --> 00:36:54,080
of information act show that Nestlé
has been approaching the government

468
00:36:54,080 --> 00:36:58,200
with its concerns. They
include this correspondence

469
00:36:58,200 --> 00:37:00,800
between the Federal
Department of Foreign Affairs

470
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:05,320
and the State Secretariat
for Economic Affairs or SECO.

471
00:37:05,320 --> 00:37:07,200
"I was able to
speak with...

472
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:09,920
who confirmed to me
the gravity of the problem

473
00:37:09,920 --> 00:37:11,880
and the urgent
need for action.

474
00:37:11,880 --> 00:37:15,000
Nestlé has approached
SECO directly to ask

475
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,800
for the support
of Swiss officials.

476
00:37:20,800 --> 00:37:25,520
SECO refused our requests
for an interview - unlike Nestlé.

477
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:32,560
We agreed with
the aim of the law,

478
00:37:32,560 --> 00:37:37,200
which was to decrease obesity
rates and help consumers eat healthy.

479
00:37:40,320 --> 00:37:44,360
What we didn't agree with was
the implementation of this law,

480
00:37:44,360 --> 00:37:49,320
because it doesn't help
consumers in their choices.

481
00:37:50,320 --> 00:37:54,520
The proposed labeling in
Mexico is merely an alarm system,

482
00:37:54,520 --> 00:37:57,720
and does not encourage
consumers to make better choices.

483
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:04,440
But isn't an alert necessary
when 76% of the population

484
00:38:04,440 --> 00:38:08,320
is overweight
or obese?

485
00:38:09,520 --> 00:38:15,840
Absolutely. You're right: we need
to set up educational programs

486
00:38:15,840 --> 00:38:18,440
to reduce obesity
in the population,

487
00:38:18,440 --> 00:38:21,760
to help people make
better consumer choices.

488
00:38:22,480 --> 00:38:25,760
So: Nestlé wants to
create support programs?

489
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:28,600
What is certain is
that the multinational

490
00:38:28,600 --> 00:38:31,560
wants to block any
statutory requirements.

491
00:38:31,560 --> 00:38:36,280
Among the messages sent by Nestlé
to the Swiss government is this memo:

492
00:38:37,480 --> 00:38:40,520
"The proposal... is too
radical and restrictive,

493
00:38:40,520 --> 00:38:43,720
and lacks
scientific rationale.

494
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:48,080
...The warning messages are susceptible
of creating unnecessary fear

495
00:38:48,080 --> 00:38:49,680
in consumers..."

496
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:57,560
We showed the memo to
Mexico's deputy minister of health.

497
00:39:00,960 --> 00:39:05,400
My first response is to
laugh. But it's a tragic laugh.

498
00:39:05,400 --> 00:39:09,880
I laugh because of the rudeness
and stubbornness of the food industry

499
00:39:09,880 --> 00:39:15,080
- in this case, a very influential
multinational corporation.

500
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:20,840
The companies have spent
many years developing this rhetoric.

501
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:25,440
I'm not surprised to see them
trying to deceive the public,

502
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,240
claiming that labelling
is not beneficial.

503
00:39:34,440 --> 00:39:39,000
Nestlé has now even improved the
formulas for a range of its products,

504
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:42,000
in order to avoid the
dreaded black labels.

505
00:39:45,160 --> 00:39:49,360
It's understandable for
companies to protect their interests.

506
00:39:49,360 --> 00:39:51,600
But economic and
private interests

507
00:39:51,600 --> 00:39:54,720
cannot be placed
above the public interest.

508
00:39:56,640 --> 00:39:59,000
That's why we talk
about separating

509
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:02,000
the economic interests
from political power.

510
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:04,640
The political power
of a sovereign nation

511
00:40:04,640 --> 00:40:09,320
involves protecting, defending
and promoting public interests.

512
00:40:15,400 --> 00:40:18,960
And chief among those
interests is protecting children.

513
00:40:18,960 --> 00:40:22,280
Nathalie Farpour-Lambert
is a pediatrician

514
00:40:22,280 --> 00:40:25,440
who's spent decades
counseling overweight minors

515
00:40:25,440 --> 00:40:29,400
- helping them in their
struggle toward a healthier diet.

516
00:40:33,920 --> 00:40:37,960
In the past, we used to have
children who were slightly overweight

517
00:40:37,960 --> 00:40:40,640
in relation to their
age and height...

518
00:40:40,640 --> 00:40:44,520
But today, we have children
who are massively overweight

519
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:47,040
- by 20 or 30
kilos or more.

520
00:40:48,360 --> 00:40:53,600
What upsets me the most is the fact
that children are being manipulated.

521
00:40:55,200 --> 00:40:59,320
They are victims of this food
system. They can't help it.

522
00:40:59,320 --> 00:41:02,960
Often, we blame the parents
for making the wrong choices

523
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:05,400
and buying
unhealthy products.

524
00:41:05,400 --> 00:41:09,840
But it's extremely difficult for
them to make the right choice.

525
00:41:09,840 --> 00:41:12,360
The labelling isn't
always clear to everyone.

526
00:41:15,880 --> 00:41:19,000
We're violating the
human rights of children,

527
00:41:19,000 --> 00:41:22,000
and you have to ask
how far things will go.

528
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:26,000
Are we willing to sacrifice
one or even two generations?

529
00:41:40,720 --> 00:41:45,240
For years now, I've been unable
to look strangers in the eye.

530
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:48,360
I've suffered so much from
the mocking and the insults

531
00:41:48,360 --> 00:41:50,760
that I've retreated
into my own bubble.

532
00:41:50,760 --> 00:41:56,080
Sometimes I don't even think of myself
as a person. I'm just this ugly thing.

533
00:41:59,040 --> 00:42:01,680
What if you didn't
have your children?

534
00:42:01,680 --> 00:42:05,840
I'd have let myself go completely
and would have been dead years ago.

535
00:42:05,840 --> 00:42:10,520
I didn't love myself enough to find an
escape. I only did it for my children,

536
00:42:10,520 --> 00:42:14,640
because I love them and want
them to have a "normal" mother.

